*3rd Cloud Futures Workshop 2012*: Hot Topics in Research and Education

May 7--8, 2012 | Berkeley, California, United States

*Website:*http://research.microsoft.com/en-US/events/cloudfutures2012/

Cloud computing is an exciting platform for research and education. It=20
has the potential to advance scientific and technological progress by=20
making data and computing resources readily available at unprecedented=20
economy of scale and nearly infinite scalability. To realize the full=20
promise of cloud computing for research and education, however, we must=20
think about the cloud as a holistic platform for creating new services,=20
new experiences, and new methods to pursue research, teaching, and=20
scholarly communication. This goal presents a broad range of interesting=20
questions.

The Cloud Futures Workshop series brings together thought leaders from=20
academia, industry, and government to discuss the role of cloud=20
computing across a variety of research and educational areas.=20
Presentations, posters and discussions will investigate how new=20
programming techniques, software platforms, software engineering and=20
methodology, and methods of research and teaching in the cloud may solve=20
distinct challenges arising in diverse areas of society.

*General Co-Chairs*

Michaael J Franklin, University of California, Berkeley

Tony Hey, Microsoft Research

*Keynote speakers*

Joseph L Hellerstein, Manager, Big Science , Google Inc.

Yousef Khalidi, Distinguished Engineer, Microsoft Corporation

*Call for Abstracts and Participation*

This year, we are looking for extended abstracts on hot topics in cloud=20
computing to be presented at the workshop---either as talks or posters.=20
Abstracts should highlight how new techniques and methods of research in=20
the cloud may solve distinct challenges arising in diverse areas,=20
including computer science, engineering, earth sciences, healthcare,=20
humanities, interactive games, life sciences, and social sciences. We=20
encourage abstracts that describe practical experiences, experimental=20
results, and vision papers. All papers will be peer-reviewed.

*Submission Instructions*

=B7Submit abstracts of five pages, including references.

=B7Your submission should include a bio (150 words maximum).

=B7Submit your abstracts through the online form=20
<https://mail.microsoft.com/owa/redir.aspx?C=3DdnvrQ6rAd065TEvukSGfg1evau=
XAqs4ImhoW0OYeMkWA_utxcf4W8nIiM7al3R8qGfAQiC3gS70.&URL=3Dhttps%3a%2f%2fcm=
t.research.microsoft.com%2fcloudfutures2012%2f>.

*Important Dates*

=B7Abstracts due: February 29, 2012

=B7Results available: March 23, 2012

=B7Workshop: May 7--8, 2012

*About the Workshop*

The Cloud Futures 2012 workshop is a joint venture between the Microsoft=20
Research Connections, Azure Research Engagement, and Developer &=20
Platform Evangelism Academic groups, and is in association with and=20
co-supported by the University of California, Berkeley.

